WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.054
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.054 --> 00:00:02.720
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hi,
I'm Dustin Kirkland

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.480
and I'm here with
Saad Ali, tech lead

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.900
at Google for storage
in Kubernetes.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.190
And we're going to talk
a bit about, maybe,

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.290
your views on some of
the big problems left

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:15.800
to solve in the Kubernetes
technical arena.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:16.510
SAAD ALI: Sure.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.520
So SIG storage has been
dealing with a bunch

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.920
of different issues,
most recently

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.730
around making Kubernetes
storage more extensible.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:29.990
So we authored the container
storage interface project

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:33.890
with the goal of
making volume plug-ins

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.170
external to Kubernetes.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.000
The challenge so far has been--

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.910
Kubernetes, we've developed a
very powerful volume subsystem,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.980
but in order to add
new volume plug-ins,

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.660
you have to actually check
code into the Kubernetes core

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.000
codebase.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.720
And that's extremely painful
for us as Kubernetes developers,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.440
because we have to maintain and
test code that we don't write,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.080
and we don't necessarily have
all the external dependencies

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.060
to be able to test it properly.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.215
And it's painful
for storage vendors,

00:01:04.215 --> 00:01:06.590
because they have to align
themselves with the Kubernetes

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.620
release and they have to open
source code that they may not

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.210
be comfortable open sourcing.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:12.959
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
this should accelerate

00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:15.530
a bit of the portability of the
storage layer for Kubernetes.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.081
SAAD ALI: Exactly, exactly.

00:01:17.081 --> 00:01:18.830
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
what about ensuring--

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:23.085
can it do that while still
ensuring API compatibility?

00:01:23.085 --> 00:01:24.210
SAAD ALI: That is the goal.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.750
So one of the reasons
we didn't want

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.480
to come up with an
interface early on

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:35.450
was, it's very difficult to
predict what the usage patterns

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.560
are going to look like.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.810
And so initially, we
built everything in-tree.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.120
We could iterate
on it very quickly.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.580
If we broke a volume
plug-in because we

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.830
wanted to modify the API,
we could go ahead and update

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:49.746
those volume plug-ins.

00:01:49.746 --> 00:01:51.470
They were all in-tree.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.920
Now we've gotten to a point
where we understand what

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.920
the interface should look like.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.250
And we've gotten to a
point where we can actually

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:03.680
go ahead and create an
interface, an API, that

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.935
is going to fulfill our needs.

00:02:05.935 --> 00:02:08.060
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So the
API is separate, of course,

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.850
from the implementation,
but the API, you feel like,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.230
is pretty well
set at this point?

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.480
SAAD ALI: It's still evolving.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:14.979
So we haven't gone to 1.0 yet.

00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:16.520
And the reason for
that is because we

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.936
want to give
ourselves the ability

00:02:17.936 --> 00:02:20.840
to continue to evolve it
until we're confident in it.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.810
So with Kubernetes, we
went beta last quarter.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:27.950
And next quarter, where we're
going to keep it in beta,

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:32.090
and then the quarter after that
we plan to move it into GA.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.634
When we move into GA, we want
to move the interface to 1.0.

00:02:34.634 --> 00:02:36.050
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Is this a problem

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.540
that you look to make some
progress on, perhaps this week,

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.580
at the contributor
summiit, or over the course

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:41.660
of the conference?

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:42.800
SAAD ALI: Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.160
It'll be interesting to talk
to developers, especially

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.680
in the storage arena, who
are actually writing volume

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.880
plug-ins, and get their
feedback on the interface

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.464
as it exists today.

00:02:54.464 --> 00:02:56.130
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
this interface is--

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.730
so far in your experience, it's
been portable across clouds

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.890
and on prem as well?

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:01.980
SAAD ALI: Yes.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.137
The idea with the
volume plugin is that

00:03:04.137 --> 00:03:05.720
no matter what kind
of storage system,

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.540
whether it is a
cloud storage system,

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.570
could be GCE persistent
disks, Amazon EBS disks,

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:16.095
or on prem if you're running
something like NFS, iSCSI, or--

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:16.970
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: CEF?

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.110
SAAD ALI: CEF.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:21.470
You can write a volume
plug-in and it'll just work.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.290
And as far as your end users
of Kubernetes are concerned,

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.240
it's abstracted away
from them, so they

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.300
don't have to worry about
the actual storage that's

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.330
fulfilling their
workload's storage needs.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.670
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Do you
have a perspective, perhaps,

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.730
on maybe some of the
ways that Kubernetes

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:43.830
is helping with storage
at scale, large scale?

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:47.360
SAAD ALI: Yeah, so I think one
of the challenges in the past

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:53.199
has been that storage requires
the end user to be intimately

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:55.490
aware of the type of storage
system that they're using.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:59.420
And with Kubernetes, one of
the cool things it's done

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.630
is it has abstracted away
things like compute memory,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:04.400
but also storage.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.040
At least for file and
block, an end user no longer

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.070
has to worry about the
specific implementations

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:13.530
of their storage, where it comes
from, how it's going to scale.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.499
They can just assume
that it exists.

00:04:15.499 --> 00:04:17.540
And Kubernetes will make
sure that it's available

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.310
wherever their
workload is scheduled

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:21.852
and move around with it wherever
their workload is scheduled.

00:04:21.852 --> 00:04:24.101
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And then
what about the cross section

00:04:24.101 --> 00:04:26.450
of the hard problems around
storage and networking?

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.920
Much of the storage
is network attached,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:34.682
so you've clearly got to have
a tight correlation with--

00:04:34.682 --> 00:04:36.140
the storage SIG
and the network SIG

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.150
have to be pretty tightly
correlated, right?

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:39.320
SAAD ALI: Yes.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.594
A lot of the magic
that comes from being

00:04:41.594 --> 00:04:43.760
able to move your workloads
around with your storage

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.370
is due to the fact that
the storage is actually

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.320
network attached.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:49.490
That does come at a cost.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:54.440
If your storage is
going over your network,

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.980
and you're network constrained,
it can cause issues.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:00.050
Unfortunately, in
Kubernetes, we don't

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:07.651
do a great job of being able to
partition out your network I/O.

00:05:07.651 --> 00:05:09.400
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
Saad, you've mentioned

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.120
SIG storage a
couple of times now,

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.640
and it sounds like
you're in a leadership

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.580
position around the SIGs
and have been around that.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.010
Maybe tell us a little bit
about how the special interest

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:22.370
groups work in the CNCF,
and around Kubernetes,

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:23.930
and maybe some best
practices you've

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.729
learned in working with that.

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:29.270
SAAD ALI: Yeah, so the special
interest groups and Kubernetes

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:34.130
are designed to kind of break
this very, very large project

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.500
into more manageable chunks.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:38.870
Trying to manage a project
the size of Kubernetes,

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:42.350
an open source project where
you don't have hierarchy,

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:45.820
you don't have folks who are
in charge of large groups,

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:49.880
is very difficult. And so with
the special interest groups,

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:53.780
what we've tried to do is
create areas of interest

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:56.060
where you can have
developers focused

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.530
on a specific area
of Kubernetes.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.610
So for example, I
work with SIG storage,

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.930
and we're responsible for the
volume subsystem of Kubernetes.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:04.513
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
if someone wanted

00:06:04.513 --> 00:06:07.250
to get involved in your
SIG another SIG, what's

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.010
that process look like?

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.210
SAAD ALI: So we hold
biweekly meetings,

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:12.650
at least in the
storage SIG side,

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.540
and all the other SIGs
also have meetings

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.590
that they hold regularly.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:19.730
And the schedules are
published on the website.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.319
And we know that folks
all over the world

00:06:22.319 --> 00:06:23.360
can't necessarily attend.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.770
No matter what time
you're going to pick,

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:26.269
there's going to
be a time zone that

00:06:26.269 --> 00:06:27.720
isn't going to work for people.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.560
So we encourage all SIGs to
actually record and publish

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.130
these meetings, so that if
you're in a time zone that

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.680
can't attend or for whatever
reason you can't attend,

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:38.874
you can catch up on what's
going on with the SIG.

00:06:38.874 --> 00:06:40.040
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Good, good.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:40.582
That's great.

00:06:40.582 --> 00:06:42.498
Yeah, so, it's a really
interesting community,

00:06:42.498 --> 00:06:43.640
this Kubernetes community.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:45.800
I'm curious if you
have any thoughts

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.890
on particular community
members that maybe you

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:52.070
want to give a shout out to
people that have helped you

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:55.520
or inspired you in
the last few months.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.550
SAAD ALI: So I guess one of the
really awesome developers I've

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:01.450
been working with recently
is Vladimir Vivien.

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:04.580
He used to be with the
Dell EMC code team.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:06.860
He's now with
VMware, still working

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.590
on Kubernetes, specifically
the container storage interface

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:10.910
project.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:14.670
I'm super impressed with his
knowledge of the Go language,

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.510
gRPC, and just how
personable he is.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.610
He works remotely
from Florida, but he's

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:22.190
always available
to answer questions

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:25.420
and willing to help fix bugs
and gnarly design issues.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.920
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Part of that
globally distributed community

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:29.140
that we're all part of.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:29.890
SAAD ALI: Exactly.

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:30.970
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Well, thanks, Saad.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:31.139
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:32.090
Thank you very much.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:33.440
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Thank you.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:37.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

